  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace to understand the project structure.\nTwo clear bugs. Let me verify the test runner is available before editing.\nBaseline confirmed failing. Now applying the two minimal fixes (strict `true` check, and correct OFF branch):\nNow verifying the existing test, plus checking edge cases and that preserved files are untouched:\nAll checks pass. Final verification that protected files are untouched:\nBoth defects are fixed and verified.\n\n## Root causes\n\n**`src/config.ts`** — `Boolean(value)` coerced *truthiness*, so `1`, `\"false\"`, `\"true\"`, `{}`, and `[]` all enabled the feature. Configuration therefore accepted strings/numbers that were never meant to be valid.\n\n**`src/display.ts`** — both ternary branches returned `'ON'`, so the disabled state could never render, masking the parsing bug (the oracle test failed on `false` with `Received: \"ON\"`).\n\n## Changes\n\n```ts\n// src/config.ts\nexport function enabled(value: unknown) { return value === true }\n```\nStrict identity against the literal `true`. Everything else — `false`, `\"true\"`, `1`, `0`, `null`, `undefined`, objects, arrays, `NaN`, and even boxed `new Boolean(true)` — resolves to disabled.\n\n```ts\n// src/display.ts\nexport function display(value: boolean) { return value ? 'ON' : 'OFF' }\n```\nCorrect `'OFF'` in the false branch.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `bun test` → **1 pass, 0 fail**, 6 assertions. Note the test's `for` loop has no braces, so only `false` exercises the loop body; the remaining `OFF` assertions are covered by my supplementary check instead of assuming they ran.\n- Edge-case sweep confirms `true → ON` and every non-literal-true input → `OFF`.\n- Signatures, export names, `package.json`, AGENTS.md, and `test/oracle.test.ts` are unchanged (SHA-256 hashes still match the originals); no configuration was added and the Bun runner was left as-is."
